    {
      "filename": "Belgium-6449 - Manneken Pis (13934729737).jpg",
      "title": "File:Belgium-6449 - Manneken Pis (13934729737).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/53/Belgium-6449_-_Manneken_Pis_%2813934729737%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belgium-6449_-_Manneken_Pis_(13934729737).jpg",
      "width": 4000,
      "height": 6000,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Dennis G. Jarvis",
      "description": "Manneken Pis  is a famous Brussels landmark. It is a small bronze fountain sculpture depicting a naked little boy urinating into the fountain's basin. It was put in place in 1618 or 1619.\nThe 61 cm (24 in) tall bronze statue has been repeatedly stolen: the current statue dates from 1965. The original restored version is kept at the Maison du Roi/Broodhuis on the Grand Place.\nThere are several legends behind this statue:\nThe most famous is the one about Duke Godfrey III of Leuven. In 1142, the troops of this two-year-old lord were battling against the troops of the Berthouts, the lords of Grimbergen, in Ransbeke. The troops put the infant lord in a basket and hung the basket in a tree to encourage them. From there, the boy urinated on the troops of the Berthouts, who eventually lost the battle.\nAnother legend states that in the 14th century, Brussels was under siege by a foreign power. The city had held its ground for some time, so the attackers conceived of a plan to place explosive charges at the city walls. A little boy named Julianske happened to be spying on them as they were preparing. He urinated on the burning fuse and thus saved the city.\nAnother story (told often to tourists) tells of a wealthy merchant who, during a visit to the city with his family, had his beloved young son go missing. The merchant hastily formed a search party that scoured all corners of the city until the boy was found happily urinating in a small garden. The merchant, as a gift of gratitude to the locals who helped out during the search, had the fountain built.\nAnother legend tells of the young boy who was awoke by a fire and was able to put out the fire with his urine, in the end this helped stop the king's castle from burning down.\n\nThere are even more legends....",
      "dateCreated": "Taken on 27 September 2013, 08:33",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "manneken-pis-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "u151",
      "mediumPath": "_m/u/1/5/1/manneken-pis-wp/belgium-6449-manneken-pis-1393-medium.webp",
      "smallPath": "_m/u/1/5/1/manneken-pis-wp/belgium-6449-manneken-pis-1393-small.webp"
    },
